message: If you use this work, please cite it as below.
title: 'Ontological Impoverishment: Reachable Bodies and the Freedom to Take Form'
authors:
- name: PIATRA Institute
date-released: '2026-11-01'
url: https://piatra.institute/papers/ontological-impoverishment.pdf
repository-code: https://github.com/piatra-institute/papers-ontological-impoverishment
abstract: This paper asks which ways of being embodied a person can reach, and defines ontological impoverishment as their foreclosure by arrangements for which a feasible, justifiable alternative exists. An embodiment is reachable when known routes lead to it through states that are physically possible, livable for the person with available support, and accessible on terms that leave its purposes under the person's control; remaining in the current embodiment is assessed separately. Three propositions follow. With institutions and routes fixed, income enlarges reachable sets only through price and purchased support, and across institutional regimes a poorer person can have the strictly larger set; relaxing one constraint helps only where the others already hold; and intermediate states of a transition count. The structure takes Heidegger's being-ahead-of-itself and thrownness and sets aside his non-quantitative notion of world-poverty. Experiments by Levin and colleagues on planarians, ectopic eyes, xenobots and anthrobots enlarge possibility and known routes, not access. The account separates More's original ability from Sandberg's negative right and locates xenofeminism's demands in the non-price conditions of access. Published Pew aggregates test one version of the claim that resistance to enhancement belongs to the political left. In unadjusted tables from November 2021, U.S. Democrats were more often excited than Republicans about all 6 techniques asked about, led by disease prevention, by 8 to 11 points, and more often feared under-regulation, as they did for non-bodily technologies. These descriptive attitudes do not measure commitment to morphological freedom.
